00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,820
Chapter seven, how skill is actually built.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:07,980
A recall from chapter six, what did repeatedly watching

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:11,100
how to videos do to people's confidence and to

4
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:11,820
their skill?

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,840
Confidence went up, actual skill did not.

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:22,080
Only a brief hands -on attempt reduced the illusion.

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,880
Deliberate practice, as Anders Ericsson defined it, means focused

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:31,260
work on specific weaknesses with feedback designed by a

9
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:32,100
skilled teacher.

10
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:35,680
It is necessary, but it explains less than the

11
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,880
10 ,000 hours story suggests.

12
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,740
Across 88 studies, deliberate practice explained about 12 %

13
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:44,280
of the differences in performance.

14
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:48,640
That varies hugely by domain, 26 % in games,

15
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:53,040
21 in music, 18 in sports, four in education,

16
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,180
and under 1 % in professions.

17
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:55,640
Americans.

18
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:59,400
Among elite athletes, it explained just 1%.

19
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,820
A double -blind repeat of the famous violinist study

20
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:06,420
with 39 violinists did not confirm the original.

21
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,640
The good group had actually logged more practice than

22
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:10,280
the best group.

23
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:12,540
Practice is one factor of many.

24
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:16,840
Ericsson's side replies that the critics measured generic practice,

25
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:21,740
and their reanalysis of strict deliberate practice estimates 29%,

26
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:25,300
rising to 61 % after statistical correction.

27
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:29,480
Either way, starting knowledge, working memory, the quality of

28
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:33,040
feedback, and how predictable the domain is all matter

29
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,260
alongside volume.

30
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:38,500
Your skills are profession -like, so better feedback loops

31
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,660
and better mental models are your levers, more than

32
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:42,420
raw hours.

33
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,860
Across 607 effect sizes, feedback improved performance by about

34
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,820
0 .4 on average, but more than a third

35
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:55,320
of feedback interventions made performance worse, especially feedback that

36
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:58,040
pulled attention toward the self rather than the task.

37
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:03,660
A 2020 meta -analysis of 435 studies found an

38
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:07,260
average of 0 .48, and the more information the

39
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:09,820
feedback contained, the bigger the effect.

40
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:12,260
Good feedback answers three questions.

41
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,540
Where am I going?

42
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:14,580
How am I going?

43
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:15,680
And where to next?

44
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,940
Feedback about the person is the least effective kind.

45
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:22,880
For your work, retention graphs, click through rates, and

46
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:25,440
a viewer saying here's where I lost interest, a

47
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,680
rich task feedback.

48
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,360
Comments praising or attacking you are weak or harmful

49
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,640
as learning signals.

50
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:35,520
Gut instinct becomes reliable only where the environment is

51
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:38,360
regular and gives fast, clear feedback.

52
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:41,240
Researchers call these kind environments.

53
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:45,840
In Wicked Ones, feedback is delayed, incomplete, or biased,

54
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,220
and people gain confidence without gaining skill.

55
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,760
No study classifies creator work this way, so this

56
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:53,860
is reasoned inference.

57
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:55,940
Technical editing is kind.

58
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:59,740
Recreate expert edits shot -for -shot with instant checks.

59
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,240
Creative editing is semi -kind.

60
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,460
Rebuild sequences and compare three editors.

61
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:08,120
AI prompting is mostly kind.

62
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:11,400
Test two or three variants on the same input.

63
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,780
Scripting and hooks are semi -kind.

64
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,740
Write daily and check 30 -second retention.

65
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,660
Ideation and packaging are semi -wicked.

66
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,120
High volume and log your predictions.

67
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:26,740
Brand building is wicked with long horizon signals and

68
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:27,800
business is wicked.

69
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,880
write hypotheses, run cheap tests, and review them with

70
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:32,440
a mentor.

71
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,020
Experts outperform others not because they have bigger working

72
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:39,080
memories, but because they have libraries of patterns.

73
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:43,400
Chessmasters recall real board positions far better than novices,

74
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:45,040
but not random ones.

75
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:48,860
For you, the patterns are hook types, story structures,

76
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:51,640
title formulas, and transition styles.

77
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:55,400
Build the library by labeling examples and practicing from

78
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,760
the labels, not by watching passively.

79
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,240
Experts also leave out roughly 70 % of their

80
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,820
decisions when they explain how they work.

81
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:06,640
So don't ask a mentor, what's your advice?

82
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:09,660
Ask, walk me through the last time you did

83
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:10,060
this.

84
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:13,480
Practice transfers mainly to closely similar tasks.

85
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:17,620
Working memory training transferred to similar tasks with an

86
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:21,140
effect of 0 .46, but to different ones at

87
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,760
only 0 .12.

88
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,660
So your practice should look like the real work,

89
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,920
real scripts, real edits, real offers.

90
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,300
Your skills are profession -like.

91
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,840
If not raw hours, what are your levers?

92
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:41,100
Better feedback loops and better mental models.

93
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:45,680
Pattern libraries built by labeling examples and practicing from

94
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:46,020
them.

95
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,940
What should you ask a mentor or instead of

96
